# API & Frontend Integration Workflow

When modifying a backend API endpoint (any C# controller, query, command, or DTO change) AND the frontend calls that API:

1. **Build the AppHost first** — running `dotnet build` on the AppHost project triggers nswag to regenerate `src/Web/FE/src/app/web-api-client.ts`. This ensures the FE has the correct typed API client.
2. **Use the generated client** — always call backend APIs from the Angular frontend via `src/Web/FE/src/app/web-api-client.ts`. Import the generated service classes and methods from this file.
3. **Never edit `web-api-client.ts` manually** — this file is auto-generated by nswag. Any manual edits will be overwritten on the next AppHost build.

### Build order
```
dotnet build src/AppHost/          # regenerates web-api-client.ts via nswag
```

### Frontend API usage example

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — use the auto-generated client
import { StepsClient } from '../../web-api-client';
constructor(private stepsClient: StepsClient) {}
this.stepsClient.getSteps().subscribe(data => { ... });

// ❌ BAD — never manually call HttpClient directly for API calls that have a generated client
// ❌ BAD — never manually edit src/Web/FE/src/app/web-api-client.ts
```
